# Exploratory Android renderer observations, 31 October 2026

These two task-only, offline release probes ran on the authorized OPD2403 tablet (Android SDK 35, Adreno 750). Compatibility and Mobile reported the expected `gl_compatibility` and `mobile` rendering methods; Android logs identified the latter as Vulkan 1.3.128 Forward Mobile. They ran sequentially with different cache and thermal histories. They are not a controlled A/B: the APK byte audit failed, generated visual revision keys differ, the Android Adreno Compatibility path changes anti-aliasing, and effective AA was not logged.

The Compatibility report completed 17/17 cases without reported workload errors. Its first Star load recorded 36,634 ms phase wall time (36,906 ms including fixture compilation) and a 2,342 ms maximum application callback interval. The repeat Star load reported 8,224 ms. These are one-run observations, not evidence of a shader cause. App startup wall time was 4,117 ms, but no application frame intervals were recorded for that phase.

The Mobile report completed 15/17 cases, with `race unavailable: star_first` and `incomplete case set: 15/17`. Its process log shows `Race preparation timed out at stage: Loading track…` at 14:31:08, after a 14:30:21 first-Star begin. The private snapshot's `RACE_LOAD_DEADLINE_SECONDS` is 45; this was the application timeout, not an observed OS kill. The subsequent Star repeat phase reported 35,909 ms but followed the failed first attempt, so it is not a matched first-load or clean repeat measurement. Mobile startup was 5,311 ms with zero recorded application-frame intervals. The cause of the timeout is unresolved.

Full observations are `device-gl/observations.jsonl`, `device-vk/observations.jsonl`, decoded analysis is `pair-analysis-v3.json`, and the Mobile PID log is `mobile-pid-logcat-partial.txt`. Gate markers were observed. SurfaceFlinger samples are raw, with ring overflow, polling gaps and unsynchronized clocks; present-gap maxima are lower bounds unless phase-boundary coverage and time alignment are verified. The probes do not trace native GL/Vulkan shader compilation, actual GPU execution, touch-to-photon latency, or driver memory.

A separate warmed instrumented pass captured screenshot, `dumpsys meminfo` and thermal-service output at gate, first-Star load and first-Star driving in `instrumented-gl/` and `instrumented-vk/`. It perturbed the device and was excluded from the first timing passes. The driving screenshots show the same general Star start area, different race time and positions, and false-start banners; they do not establish pixel parity. The driving snapshots reported total PSS 527,209 KiB for Compatibility and 1,226,914 KiB for Mobile, neither peak nor GPU allocation; states, AA and order differ, so these values do not isolate renderer cost. Snapshot files may contain system thermal/service information. No production app data was cleared; both isolated packages were left installed for the separate trace.

After the separate trace and evidence verification, both isolated packages were uninstalled. The production package remained installed; `device-cleanup-receipt.json` retains command results and pre-cleanup artifact hashes. Its hash for this report records the file before this cleanup note was added.

The false-start banners are an additional **driver-workload limitation** in the separate instrumented runs. The probe assigns its automatic driver before countdown finishes, and the game's launch logic can penalize an early start. Neither primary report records the launch result, and neither primary `observations.jsonl` nor the available Mobile PID log includes a false-start message; therefore the primary runs' false-start status is unknown. The primary reports record distance, collisions and driver samples, but those do not prove identical trajectories, starting behavior, or complete race workloads. The Mobile primary first Star race did not start at all.
